okay call this one GDP served Italian  style Italy is changing how it  calculates its gross domestic product a  measurement of the overall economy to  include the black market everything from  prostitution to arms trafficking to  illegal drug sales economists predict  the illegal sales will add one point  three percent to GDP this year hey it's  one way to boost your growth right but  it could also backfire and after all the  criticism Italy received for former  prime minister silvio berlusconi's  indiscretions and fraud conviction this  won't help the country's image by  including the black market for which  there are no concrete ways to measure  and accurately determine value the  Italian government is going to be able  to calculate its GDP numbers in a way  that is bound to open it to criticism  and at least annoy its northern  neighbors simultaneously investors will  learn to dismiss or to discount at the  very least Italy's statistics since they  won't be regarded as real now the reason  Italy is becoming creative with its  accounting is because it wants to and  the European Union needs it to look  better on paper after all a larger  overall economy will enable Italy to  lower its debt to GDP ratio which is of  course an essential part of meeting the  e use financial standards so  theoretically this should enable Italy  to borrow more money you can think of it  like this if a family's annual income  goes up it would qualify for a bigger  mortgage though if income went up  because the family robbed a bank the  family's creditworthiness wouldn't  exactly be enhanced now while Italy's  move may be considered on Orthodox even  shady it's definitely not illegal in  fact the EU encourages this creative  accounting in order to give countries  like Italy a lifeline new EU rules  require member states to include the  value of all income producing activity  in GDP calculations and illegal activity  is often an income-producing activity  Italy is the first and only country  though to take advantage of this so far  but others like Spain for which twenty  percent of the overall economy is  believed to be black market and Greece  may soon follow the beauty of the black  market at least if you're the borrower  is that its value can be interpreted  however you want so in the US for  example the illegal marijuana business  estimated to be worth between 10 billion  and a hundred billion dollars that is a  pretty big spread and it all has to do  with how an economist actually measures  those numbers so if you're an economist  in Italy who needs to make your economy  look better you'll want to go with those  liberal calculations but the truth is no  one really knows bottom line here the  real danger in padding GDP with black  market activity is that it will cement  the distinction between the more austere  outlook of Northern Europe with the  so-called la dolce vita lifestyle of the  southern neighbors you can read more in  my USA Today op-ed column out today
 

